from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much do you already know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The philosopher whose teachings on ethics, family duty, and good government shaped Chinese society for over two thousand years is known in pinyin as…","options":["Laozi","Kongzi (Confucius)","Mozi","Hanfeizi"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kongzi, Latinized as Confucius, founded the tradition later called Confucianism — see ''The philosophical foundations.''"},{"prompt":"Chinese is written with a system based mainly on…","options":["an alphabet of 26 letters","characters, each standing for a syllable and a meaning","a set of only five symbols","pure pictures with no sound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each character (hanzi) carries both a sound and a meaning; it is not alphabetic — see ''The Chinese language and writing system.''"},{"prompt":"The most important festival in the Chinese calendar, marking the start of a new lunar year, is…","options":["the Mid-Autumn Festival","the Dragon Boat Festival","the Spring Festival (Lunar New Year)","the Lantern Festival"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Spring Festival, or Lunar New Year (Chunjie), is the year''s largest celebration — see ''Festivals and traditions.''"},{"prompt":"The Chinese zodiac assigns to each year an animal drawn from a cycle of how many animals?","options":["5","10","12","24"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Twelve animals rotate in order, from Rat to Pig — see ''Festivals and traditions.''"},{"prompt":"Which of these is one of the ''Four Great Classical Novels'' of Chinese literature?","options":["The Tale of Genji","Journey to the West (Xiyouji)","The Analects","The Art of War"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Journey to the West is one of the four great novels — see ''Literature and poetry.''"},{"prompt":"The teaching that values living in harmony with the natural way, associated with Laozi, is called…","options":["Legalism","Daoism (Taoism)","Mohism","Confucianism"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Daoism centers on the Dao, ''the way'' — see ''The philosophical foundations.''"},{"prompt":"The virtue of deep respect and care for one''s parents and ancestors, central to Chinese family life, is called…","options":["guanxi","filial piety (xiao)","ren","feng shui"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Filial piety, xiao, is a cornerstone value — see ''Family, values, and society.''"},{"prompt":"The highly stylized traditional theater combining singing, mime, acrobatics, and painted faces is best known as…","options":["Noh theater","Peking opera (jingju)","Kathakali","shadow puppetry"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Peking opera, jingju, is China''s most famous operatic form — see ''Visual arts, music, and opera.''"}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'The Chinese world and its geography', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to place Chinese civilization on the map, name the great rivers and regions that shaped it, and explain why geography matters for understanding the culture that follows. You will see why early farming communities grew up where they did, why the land was both a cradle and a fortress, and how distance and terrain produced striking regional differences in food, speech, and custom. Warm-up from the diagnostic: the Chinese name for the country, Zhongguo, is often translated as the ''Middle Kingdom.'' What does that phrase suggest about how the civilization once saw its own place in the world? Hold that thought as you read, because the idea of a central, settled agricultural heartland ringed by mountains, deserts, and steppe runs through everything from philosophy to festivals."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"China occupies a vast area of East Asia, roughly the size of a continent, and its culture grew from fertile river valleys. Two rivers stand out. The Huang He, or Yellow River, in the north carried loess soil ideal for early farming and is often called the cradle of Chinese civilization. The Chang Jiang, or Yangtze, in the center and south is the longest river in Asia and fed rice agriculture. Mountains, the Gobi Desert, and the high Tibetan Plateau form natural frontiers, while the eastern coast opens onto the Pacific. This setting encouraged a large, inward-looking agrarian society that prized stability and flood control. Geography also created diversity: the dry wheat-and-millet north differs from the wet rice-growing south in food, architecture, and dialect. Over millennia a single written language helped bind these regions together even when spoken tongues diverged, giving China a remarkable cultural continuity across thousands of years and enormous distances."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"Geography in China is tied to legend and long effort. The mythic emperor Yu the Great, Da Yu, is remembered for taming the floods of the Yellow River by digging channels rather than building dams, a story that casts good government as skilled water management. Later rulers organized huge public works along the same logic: the Grand Canal, Da Yunhe, linked the rice-rich south to the political north, and the many walls later joined into the Great Wall, Changcheng, marked the farming heartland off from the northern steppe. The classic geography text Shanhaijing, the ''Classic of Mountains and Seas,'' mixed real landscape with myth and shows how early writers mapped their world. Keep these in mind: rivers to farm by, canals to connect, and walls to defend were the three great responses to a demanding land, and each left a deep mark on Chinese values and art."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check yourself. 1) Which river is often called the cradle of Chinese civilization? 2) Which river is the longest in Asia and is tied to rice farming? 3) What does the pinyin name Zhongguo literally suggest? 4) Name one natural barrier that helped isolate the Chinese heartland. 5) What single feature helped unite regions that spoke very different dialects? Answers: 1) The Huang He, or Yellow River, in the north. 2) The Chang Jiang, or Yangtze, in the center and south. 3) ''Middle'' or ''central'' country, the Middle Kingdom. 4) Any of the Gobi Desert, the Tibetan Plateau, or the surrounding mountain ranges. 5) The shared written language, whose characters carried meaning regardless of how a reader pronounced them. If you missed the last one, it is worth remembering, because it explains much of China''s cultural continuity."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating China as one uniform place. In reality the wheat-eating, millet-growing north and the rice-growing south differ sharply in food, building styles, and speech, and that variety is part of the story. Mistake: assuming the Yangtze is the Yellow River; the Yellow River is the northern cradle, while the longer Yangtze runs through the center and south. Mistake: reading ''Middle Kingdom'' as mere arrogance rather than a description of a central farming heartland ringed by frontiers. Exit ticket: (a) Which two rivers shaped early Chinese civilization, and roughly where does each flow? (b) Give one reason a single written language mattered for such a large country. Answers: (a) the Yellow River in the north and the Yangtze in the center and south; (b) it let people across many dialects share texts, laws, and learning, knitting the regions together over long distances and long centuries."}]}'::jsonb, 'The country''s full name in pinyin is Zhongguo, often translated as the ''Middle Kingdom'' — zhong means middle or central, guo means country.', 12),
  (3, 'The philosophical foundations', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"By the end of this lesson you will be able to tell apart the four streams of thought that shaped Chinese civilization: Confucianism, Daoism, Buddhism, and Legalism. You will know what question each one tried to answer, the key figure behind each, and how they could coexist in one society and even in one person. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you met Kongzi, known in the West as Confucius. What was his main concern? As you read, notice that these are not rival religions competing for converts so much as different tools for different parts of life: how to behave in the family and the state, how to live in tune with nature, how to face suffering and death, and how to run a government firmly. Chinese thinkers have long borrowed from more than one at once."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Confucianism, from Kongzi in roughly the sixth to fifth century BCE, teaches that a good society rests on virtue, learning, ritual propriety (li), and humane feeling (ren), expressed through proper relationships such as parent and child or ruler and subject. Daoism, linked to Laozi and the text Daodejing, urges living in harmony with the Dao, the natural way, through simplicity and wu wei, ''effortless action'' that does not force things. Buddhism arrived from India along the Silk Road in the first centuries CE and addressed suffering, impermanence, and rebirth; it blended with local thought to form distinctly Chinese schools such as Chan, known elsewhere as Zen. Legalism, associated with Hanfeizi, argued that firm laws, rewards, and punishments, not virtue alone, keep order; it guided the first unification of China. Together these four gave Chinese life its ethics, its sense of nature, its spiritual depth, and its statecraft."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"Kongzi''s sayings were gathered by students into the Lunyu, the Analects, a short book of great influence; the later thinker Mengzi, or Mencius, argued that human nature is basically good. For Daoism, Laozi is the traditional author of the Daodejing, and Zhuangzi wrote witty parables, including his famous dream of being a butterfly, about the limits of fixed knowledge. Buddhism''s spread owed much to the monk Xuanzang, whose seventh-century journey to India to collect scriptures later inspired the novel Journey to the West. For Legalism, Hanfeizi collected its arguments, and the minister Shang Yang put strict law into practice in the state of Qin. Remember the pairings: Kongzi with the Analects and ren, Laozi with the Daodejing and wu wei, Buddhism with the Silk Road and Chan, and Hanfeizi with law and the rise of the Qin."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Try these, then check. 1) Which tradition stresses virtue, learning, and proper relationships? 2) What does wu wei mean, and which tradition teaches it? 3) Which tradition came to China from India along the Silk Road? 4) Which tradition holds that strict laws and punishments keep order? 5) Name the short book that collects the sayings of Kongzi. Answers: 1) Confucianism. 2) ''Effortless action,'' acting in tune with the natural way rather than forcing outcomes; it belongs to Daoism. 3) Buddhism. 4) Legalism. 5) The Analects, or Lunyu. Bonus: the Chinese Buddhist school known abroad as Zen is called Chan in pinyin. If you confused Daoism and Buddhism, recall that Daoism is native and centers on the Dao, while Buddhism is imported and centers on ending suffering."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the four traditions as mutually exclusive. Historically a scholar might serve the state as a Confucian, retreat to nature as a Daoist, and seek comfort from Buddhism, all without contradiction. Mistake: calling Kongzi a god; he taught ethics and good conduct, not worship of himself, though later generations honored him deeply. Mistake: thinking Legalism simply vanished; its emphasis on law and administration left lasting marks on Chinese government even after Confucian ideals became official. Exit ticket: (a) Match each tradition to its core concern: family and state ethics, harmony with nature, ending suffering, firm law. (b) Who is traditionally credited with the Daodejing? Answers: (a) Confucianism to ethics of family and state, Daoism to harmony with nature, Buddhism to ending suffering, Legalism to firm law; (b) Laozi."}]}'::jsonb, 'In pinyin the surname comes first: Kongzi is the master (zi) of the Kong family. ''Confucius'' is a Latinized form coined by later European writers.', 14),
  (4, 'The Chinese language and writing system', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson lets you explain how written Chinese works, why it differs from an alphabet, and how it connects to the art of calligraphy. You will understand what a character is, how characters combine sound and meaning, and why the same script can be read across regions that speak differently. Warm-up from the diagnostic: Chinese is written with characters rather than letters. What advantage might that give a country with many spoken dialects? As you read, notice two separate things that are easy to confuse: the spoken language, with its many varieties and its tones, and the written script, which is shared. The writing system is one of the oldest still in use, and learning even a little about it unlocks much of Chinese art, poetry, and daily life."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A Chinese character, hanzi, generally stands for one syllable and one unit of meaning. Unlike letters, which spell out sounds, characters carry meaning directly, which is why readers of different dialects, or even of Japanese kanji, can often grasp a written text they could not say aloud the same way. Thousands of characters exist, though a few thousand cover most everyday use. Many are formed by combining a meaning part, a radical, with a sound part, giving a hint to both sense and pronunciation. Spoken Chinese is tonal: the same syllable said with a different pitch means a different word, as pinyin''s tone marks show. The main spoken standard today is Mandarin, Putonghua, based on northern speech, while Cantonese, Yue, and other varieties remain widely used. To help teaching and typing, pinyin spells the sounds with Roman letters, and simplified characters, adopted on the mainland, reduce the strokes of many traditional forms."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"The oldest known Chinese writing appears on ''oracle bones,'' jiaguwen, from the Shang dynasty, where questions to ancestors were carved into bone and shell. The script was later standardized under the first emperor''s minister Li Si, a step that helped unify the country. The scholar Xu Shen compiled the Shuowen Jiezi, an early dictionary that analyzed how characters are built from parts. Calligraphy, shufa, turned writing into a fine art: the Eastern Jin master Wang Xizhi is honored as the ''Sage of Calligraphy'' for works such as the Lantingji Xu, the ''Orchid Pavilion Preface.'' Brush, ink, inkstone, and paper, together called the ''Four Treasures of the Study,'' are the tools of this art. Remember: oracle bones for the earliest script, Li Si for standardization, Xu Shen for the first great dictionary, and Wang Xizhi for calligraphy."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) Does a Chinese character usually stand for a sound only, or for a syllable with a meaning? 2) Why can speakers of different dialects often read the same text? 3) What is pinyin used for? 4) Name the tonal spoken standard based on northern speech. 5) What art turns writing into a prized visual form, and who is called its ''Sage''? Answers: 1) For a syllable that also carries meaning, not a bare sound. 2) Because the script encodes meaning rather than one fixed pronunciation, so it works across dialects. 3) Spelling Chinese sounds with Roman letters, used for teaching, typing, and tone marking. 4) Mandarin, in pinyin Putonghua. 5) Calligraphy, shufa; Wang Xizhi is honored as its Sage. If you missed number two, reread ''The idea,'' since that feature explains much of China''s long unity."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling Chinese writing an alphabet. It is not; characters are not letters and do not spell sounds the way a, b, c do. Mistake: thinking there is a single ''Chinese language'' spoken one way everywhere; the written script is shared, but spoken varieties such as Mandarin and Cantonese differ greatly. Mistake: ignoring tones, which carry meaning, so the same syllable at a different pitch is a different word. Mistake: confusing pinyin, a spelling tool with Roman letters, with the characters themselves. Exit ticket: (a) In one sentence, how does a character differ from an alphabetic letter? (b) Name the Four Treasures of the Study used in calligraphy. Answers: (a) a letter spells a sound while a character stands for a syllable together with a meaning; (b) brush, ink, inkstone, and paper."}]}'::jsonb, 'Pinyin is the official system for spelling Chinese sounds with Roman letters; the tone marks over vowels (as in Beijing, Beijing) show pitch, which can change a word''s meaning.', 13),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The river often called the cradle of Chinese civilization is the…","options":["Chang Jiang (Yangtze)","Huang He (Yellow River)","Mekong","Pearl River"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Huang He, or Yellow River, in the north is the traditional cradle; the Yangtze is longer but tied more to rice farming."},{"prompt":"Which tradition teaches wu wei, living in harmony with the natural way?","options":["Confucianism","Legalism","Daoism","Buddhism"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wu wei, ''effortless action,'' is a Daoist idea linked to Laozi and the Daodejing."},{"prompt":"The short book that gathers the sayings of Kongzi (Confucius) is the…","options":["Daodejing","Analects (Lunyu)","Shuowen Jiezi","Shanhaijing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Analects, Lunyu, collects his teachings on virtue and conduct."},{"prompt":"A Chinese character (hanzi) generally represents…","options":["a single consonant","a syllable that also carries meaning","a whole sentence","only a tone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each character pairs one syllable with a unit of meaning; it is not alphabetic."},{"prompt":"Which tradition reached China from India along the Silk Road and shaped the Chan (Zen) school?","options":["Daoism","Legalism","Buddhism","Mohism"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Buddhism came from India and blended with local thought to form Chan, known abroad as Zen."},{"prompt":"The calligrapher honored as the ''Sage of Calligraphy'' for the Orchid Pavilion Preface is…","options":["Li Si","Xu Shen","Wang Xizhi","Xuanzang"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wang Xizhi of the Eastern Jin is honored with that title; Li Si standardized the script and Xu Shen wrote an early dictionary."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Literature and poetry', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson will let you sketch the main shape of Chinese literature: the ancient classics, the golden age of poetry under the Tang dynasty, and the four great novels that crowned later storytelling. You will be able to name leading poets and works and say why each matters. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you recognized Journey to the West as one of the Four Great Classical Novels. What might it mean that a whole culture agrees on a short list of ''great'' novels? As you read, notice how literature in China grew out of earlier traditions of scholarship and song, how poetry became a skill expected of every educated person, and how prose fiction, once looked down upon, rose to become some of the best-loved writing in the language."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Chinese literature begins with revered classics, among them the Shijing, the ''Book of Songs,'' an anthology of early poems, and the histories and philosophical texts that scholars studied for the civil-service examinations. Poetry became the most prestigious form, reaching its height during the Tang dynasty, when verse was woven into daily and official life and tested in exams. Tang poems are prized for compact imagery, balance, and feeling, often evoking nature, friendship, exile, or the passing of time. Later, under the Ming and Qing, long prose novels flourished in the everyday language. Tradition names four as the greatest: Romance of the Three Kingdoms, Water Margin, Journey to the West, and Dream of the Red Chamber. Alongside these ran a rich tradition of essays, drama, and short tales. Together, classic anthologies, Tang verse, and the four novels form the backbone of China''s literary heritage and still shape film, opera, and popular culture today."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"The Tang produced two towering poets: Li Bai, famous for soaring, wine-loving, imaginative verse, and Du Fu, honored for moral seriousness and poems of war and hardship; Wang Wei added quiet, painterly nature poems. Among the four great novels, Romance of the Three Kingdoms, attributed to Luo Guanzhong, dramatizes the wars after the Han collapse; Water Margin, linked to Shi Nai''an, follows a band of outlaw heroes; Journey to the West, by Wu Cheng''en, turns the monk Xuanzang''s pilgrimage into fantasy with the Monkey King, Sun Wukong; and Dream of the Red Chamber, by Cao Xueqin, traces the decline of a great family with deep psychological insight. Remember the pairings: Li Bai and Du Fu for Tang poetry, and the quartet of novels with their authors for the age of fiction."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) Which dynasty is the golden age of Chinese poetry? 2) Name the two most celebrated Tang poets. 3) Which great novel features the Monkey King, Sun Wukong? 4) Name the early anthology called the ''Book of Songs.'' 5) Which novel traces the decline of a great family and is praised for psychological depth? Answers: 1) The Tang dynasty. 2) Li Bai and Du Fu. 3) Journey to the West, by Wu Cheng''en. 4) The Shijing. 5) Dream of the Red Chamber, by Cao Xueqin. Bonus: the four great novels are Romance of the Three Kingdoms, Water Margin, Journey to the West, and Dream of the Red Chamber. If you mixed up the poets, a useful tag is that Li Bai soars while Du Fu grieves."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking poetry was a niche pastime. In imperial China writing fine verse was expected of educated people and tested in exams, so it sat at the center of elite life. Mistake: assuming the great novels are ancient; they are later works, mostly of the Ming and Qing, written in more everyday language. Mistake: crediting the wrong author or hero to a novel. Keep Journey to the West with Wu Cheng''en and the Monkey King. Mistake: treating the classics and the novels as the same tradition; the classics were studied, while fiction was long dismissed before winning respect. Exit ticket: (a) Name the Four Great Classical Novels. (b) Give one reason poetry held such high status. Answers: (a) Romance of the Three Kingdoms, Water Margin, Journey to the West, and Dream of the Red Chamber; (b) it was a mark of education, valued at court, and tested in the examinations."}]}'::jsonb, 'Dynasty names often serve as adjectives for an era''s art: ''Tang poetry'' means poetry of the Tang dynasty, roughly the seventh to tenth centuries CE.', 14),
  (7, 'Visual arts, music, and opera', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson will let you describe the major Chinese visual arts, painting, ceramics, and jade, and the great performing art of Peking opera. You will know what each form values and recognize a few signatures, such as the role of empty space in a landscape painting or the painted faces of the opera stage. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you identified Peking opera as theater that fuses singing, mime, acrobatics, and painted faces. Why might a culture value a single art that blends so many skills at once? As you read, notice a shared thread: Chinese art often prizes suggestion over display, harmony over noise, and mastery of a disciplined technique, whether the artist wields a brush, shapes clay, carves jade, or trains for years to sing and tumble on stage."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"In painting, the most admired tradition is ink-and-brush landscape, shanshui, meaning ''mountains and water,'' where empty space is as important as the painted forms and the aim is to capture a scene''s spirit rather than copy it exactly. The same brush skill links painting to calligraphy and poetry, often combined on one scroll. Ceramics made China world-famous: fine porcelain, especially blue-and-white ware, was so associated with the country that porcelain is still called ''china'' in English. Jade, yu, was treasured above gold for its hardness, soft glow, and links to virtue and immortality, and was carved into ornaments and ritual objects. In music and theater, Peking opera, jingju, rose in the nineteenth century as a grand synthesis: stylized singing, spoken parts, mime, acrobatics, symbolic costumes, and painted faces whose colors signal a character''s nature. Traditional instruments such as the pipa, a lute, and the erhu, a two-stringed fiddle, carry the melodies."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"Landscape painting reached early heights with masters such as Fan Kuan of the Song dynasty, whose towering mountain scenes show the shanshui ideal. The scholar-painter tradition prized amateurs who painted for self-cultivation rather than sale. In ceramics, the kilns of Jingdezhen became the heart of porcelain making, and blue-and-white ware produced there was exported across the world. Jade carving stretches back to Neolithic cultures such as the Liangzhu, whose ritual disks and tubes survive. In opera, Mei Lanfang became the most celebrated performer of the twentieth century, famous for playing female roles, the dan, and he helped introduce Peking opera to audiences abroad. Remember the anchors: shanshui landscape and Fan Kuan for painting, Jingdezhen for porcelain, Liangzhu jade for carving, and Mei Lanfang for Peking opera."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) What does shanshui mean, and which art does it name? 2) Why is porcelain called ''china'' in English? 3) Which stone was treasured above gold and linked to virtue? 4) Name the four or more skills blended in Peking opera. 5) Which performer is the best-known Peking opera star of the twentieth century? Answers: 1) ''Mountains and water,'' the term for ink-and-brush landscape painting. 2) Because fine porcelain was so closely tied to China that its name became the word for the ware. 3) Jade, yu. 4) Singing, spoken parts, mime, acrobatics, and symbolic costume and face paint. 5) Mei Lanfang, famed for dan roles. If you forgot the role of empty space, recall that shanshui leaves much of the paper blank on purpose, to suggest mist, distance, and spirit."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: judging a landscape painting as unfinished because of its blank areas; that empty space is deliberate and carries meaning. Mistake: thinking face paint in Peking opera is mere decoration; the colors are a code, telling the audience a character''s temperament at a glance. Mistake: treating porcelain as a minor craft; it was a major export that shaped global trade and gave the English language a word. Mistake: assuming jade was valued only for looks; it also carried moral and spiritual meaning. Exit ticket: (a) What is the aim of a shanshui painting, and how does empty space serve it? (b) Name two traditional instruments heard in Chinese music. Answers: (a) to capture the spirit of a scene rather than copy it exactly, with empty space suggesting mist, depth, and mood; (b) the pipa (a lute) and the erhu (a two-stringed fiddle)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Peking opera is written jingju in pinyin: jing for the capital (Beijing) and ju for drama or play.', 13),
  (8, 'Key terms', 'vocab', '{"items":[{"term":"ren","translation":"humaneness or benevolence; the core Confucian virtue of caring for others","example":"A ruler who governs with ren wins loyalty through kindness rather than fear."},{"term":"Dao","translation":"''the way''; the natural order with which Daoists seek to live in harmony","example":"Laozi taught that forcing events disturbs the Dao, so the wise act with wu wei."},{"term":"xiao","translation":"filial piety; deep respect and care for one''s parents and ancestors","example":"Honoring grandparents and tending the family tombs are expressions of xiao."},{"term":"hanzi","translation":"a Chinese character, standing for a syllable together with a meaning","example":"A single hanzi can be read differently across dialects yet mean the same thing."},{"term":"guanxi","translation":"the web of personal relationships and mutual obligation that smooths social and business life","example":"Years of shared favors had built the guanxi that made the partnership possible."},{"term":"jingju","translation":"Peking opera; stylized theater blending song, mime, acrobatics, and painted faces","example":"In jingju a red painted face marks a loyal and courageous character."}]}'::jsonb, null, 9),
  (9, 'Festivals, traditions, food, and tea', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you will be able to name the major Chinese festivals and what they celebrate, explain the twelve-animal zodiac, and describe the broad shape of Chinese food and tea culture. You will see how eating, drinking, and celebrating are bound up with family, the seasons, and good fortune. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you placed the Spring Festival as the year''s biggest celebration and recalled that the zodiac cycles through twelve animals. What might it mean that the most important holiday is centered on returning home to family? As you read, notice a recurring theme: Chinese traditions tie the calendar to the harvest and the moon, pair food with symbolism and reunion, and treat tea not just as a drink but as a social ritual with centuries of refinement behind it."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The Spring Festival, or Lunar New Year, Chunjie, is the most important holiday, a time to reunite with family, clean the house, give red envelopes of money, and wish for prosperity in the year ahead. Each year is named for one of twelve zodiac animals, from Rat to Pig, which rotate in a fixed cycle. The Mid-Autumn Festival celebrates the full harvest moon with mooncakes and family gatherings, while other festivals mark the lanterns of the new year, the dragon-boat races of early summer, and the tending of ancestral graves in spring. Food is central and regional: the north favors wheat noodles and dumplings, the south favors rice, and famous cooking styles such as Sichuan, known for bold spice, and Cantonese, known for fresh, delicate flavor, show the range. Tea, cha, grown and drunk for thousands of years, is both an everyday comfort and a refined art, from green and oolong to the aged dark teas."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"Chinese food and tea have their classics and their customs. The Tang-dynasty writer Lu Yu composed the Chajing, the ''Classic of Tea,'' the first known book devoted to the plant, its brewing, and its spirit, and he is honored as the ''Sage of Tea.'' Festival foods carry meaning: mooncakes at Mid-Autumn recall the full moon and reunion, sticky rice dumplings, zongzi, mark the Dragon Boat Festival, and a whole fish at New Year signals surplus, because the word for fish, yu, sounds like the word for abundance. Dim sum, the small Cantonese dishes shared over tea, grew from teahouse culture. The zodiac animals appear in countless stories and decorations. Remember: Lu Yu and the Chajing for tea, zongzi for the Dragon Boat Festival, mooncakes for Mid-Autumn, and lucky food words such as fish for abundance."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) What is the most important Chinese festival, and what is its pinyin name? 2) How many animals are in the zodiac cycle? 3) Which festival is celebrated with mooncakes? 4) Name the Tang author of the ''Classic of Tea.'' 5) Why is a whole fish served at New Year? Answers: 1) The Spring Festival, or Lunar New Year, Chunjie. 2) Twelve, from Rat to Pig. 3) The Mid-Autumn Festival. 4) Lu Yu, honored as the Sage of Tea, who wrote the Chajing. 5) Because the word for fish, yu, sounds like the word for abundance, so it wishes for surplus in the year ahead. If you missed the zodiac count, remember it runs on a repeating cycle of twelve, with a new animal each year."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling the Spring Festival ''Chinese Christmas.'' It is its own tradition, centered on family reunion, the lunar calendar, and renewal, not on any Western holiday. Mistake: assuming all Chinese food is the same; regional styles such as spicy Sichuan and delicate Cantonese differ greatly, and the north-south split between wheat and rice runs deep. Mistake: treating tea as a plain beverage; it has centuries of ritual, grading, and literature behind it. Mistake: forgetting that festival foods are symbolic, not random. Exit ticket: (a) Match each to its festival: mooncakes, zongzi (sticky rice dumplings), red envelopes. (b) Why does serving fish at New Year carry good luck? Answers: (a) mooncakes with the Mid-Autumn Festival, zongzi with the Dragon Boat Festival, red envelopes with the Spring Festival; (b) the word for fish, yu, sounds like the word for abundance, making it a wish for surplus."}]}'::jsonb, 'Many festivals follow the lunar calendar, so their dates shift each year against the Western calendar; the Spring Festival is written Chunjie in pinyin.', 13),
  (10, 'Family, values, and modern Chinese culture', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This final lesson will let you explain the values at the heart of Chinese family and social life, filial piety and guanxi among them, and describe how Chinese culture has changed and spread in the modern world. You will be able to connect the traditions from earlier lessons to life today, in China and in the global Chinese community. Warm-up from the diagnostic: you met filial piety, xiao, the respect owed to parents and ancestors. How might such a value shape daily decisions, from career to care of the elderly? As you read, notice that modern Chinese culture is not a break from the past but a conversation with it: old ideas of family, harmony, and learning persist and adapt alongside rapid change, new media, and a growing worldwide influence in film, food, technology, and the arts."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"The family has long been the basic unit of Chinese society, and filial piety, xiao, the duty to honor and care for parents and ancestors, anchors it. Respect flows along age and generation, ancestors are remembered, and the good of the family often comes before the individual. Beyond the household, guanxi, the web of personal relationships and mutual obligation, helps people get things done in social and business life through trust and reciprocal favors. Harmony, education, and hard work are widely prized. In the modern era, China has undergone sweeping change, from the end of the empire and decades of upheaval to swift economic growth and urban life. Traditional values now coexist with global pop culture, digital media, and new family patterns. Meanwhile Chinese culture reaches the world: its cuisine is eaten everywhere, its films, martial arts, and festivals draw global audiences, and a large overseas Chinese diaspora carries traditions across continents while blending them with local life."},{"heading":"Key figures and works","text":"Modern Chinese culture has its landmark figures. The writer Lu Xun, in the early twentieth century, used fiction and essays to question old customs and spark reform, and is often called the father of modern Chinese literature. In film, directors such as Zhang Yimou brought Chinese stories to world screens, and the martial-arts star Bruce Lee, Li Xiaolong, made kung fu a global phenomenon. Festivals such as the Spring Festival are now marked in cities worldwide, and Chinese food, tea, and traditional medicine have spread far beyond the country. Confucian ideas about education and family still shape attitudes across East Asia and the diaspora. Remember these anchors: Lu Xun for modern literature and reform, Zhang Yimou and Bruce Lee for film and martial arts abroad, and the overseas diaspora for carrying festivals, food, and values around the globe."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"Answer, then check. 1) What is filial piety, and what is its pinyin name? 2) What does guanxi describe? 3) Which writer is called the father of modern Chinese literature? 4) Name one way Chinese culture reaches a global audience today. 5) Name the martial-arts star, known in pinyin as Li Xiaolong, who spread kung fu worldwide. Answers: 1) The duty to respect and care for one''s parents and ancestors; in pinyin, xiao. 2) The network of personal relationships and mutual obligation that smooths social and business life. 3) Lu Xun. 4) Any of: cuisine, film, martial arts, festivals such as the Spring Festival, tea, or traditional medicine. 5) Bruce Lee. If you blurred xiao and guanxi, remember xiao is about the family line, while guanxi is about the wider web of personal ties."},{"heading":"Common mistakes and exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating guanxi as mere bribery; at heart it is about trust, reputation, and reciprocal favors built over time, though it can be misused. Mistake: assuming modernization erased tradition; old values of family, harmony, and learning adapt and persist alongside rapid change. Mistake: thinking Chinese culture is confined to China; a large diaspora and global interest in its food, film, and festivals give it worldwide reach. Mistake: reducing filial piety to simple obedience; it is a broad ethic of care, respect, and remembrance across generations. Exit ticket: (a) In one sentence each, define filial piety (xiao) and guanxi. (b) Give one example of Chinese culture''s global influence today. Answers: (a) xiao is the duty to honor and care for parents and ancestors, while guanxi is the network of personal relationships and mutual obligation in social and business life; (b) examples include worldwide Chinese cuisine, films and martial arts, and Spring Festival celebrations held in cities around the globe."}]}'::jsonb, 'Guanxi literally joins guan (to connect or close) and xi (a tie or system); it names the network of relationships that shapes social and business life.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000015' and l.position = v.pos
);
